WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:14.580
&gt;&gt; Well, thank you Tuomas
for being here today.

00:00:14.581 --> 00:00:15.950
It's great to see you.

00:00:15.951 --> 00:00:17.170
&gt;&gt; My pleasure to be here again.

00:00:17.171 --> 00:00:18.780
&gt;&gt; It's great to
have you come in and

00:00:18.781 --> 00:00:19.885
share your thoughts with us

00:00:19.886 --> 00:00:21.640
at the Microsoft Research AI

00:00:21.641 --> 00:00:23.610
distinguished lecture series.

00:00:23.611 --> 00:00:25.760
We've known each other
for a very long time.

00:00:25.761 --> 00:00:27.945
I remember when I think when
you were a grad student

00:00:27.946 --> 00:00:29.770
asking me all these
questions that

00:00:29.771 --> 00:00:32.850
informs at the
international meeting

00:00:32.851 --> 00:00:35.365
of Operations Research
and Management Science.

00:00:35.366 --> 00:00:37.550
I turned around and said, "
Who's this really smart guy

00:00:37.551 --> 00:00:39.215
asking me all these questions?"

00:00:39.216 --> 00:00:44.490
I first met Tuomas that
way and what I really was

00:00:44.491 --> 00:00:46.860
excited about was
it seemed we shared

00:00:46.861 --> 00:00:49.900
interests in operations research,

00:00:49.901 --> 00:00:53.670
decision analysis, AI, the span,

00:00:53.671 --> 00:00:55.620
the arc of concepts
to all those fields,

00:00:55.621 --> 00:00:58.720
which only more recently
have come together,

00:00:58.721 --> 00:01:03.005
for the current viewpoint

00:01:03.006 --> 00:01:05.540
and perspective on
machine intelligence.

00:01:05.541 --> 00:01:07.200
So, that was [inaudible].

00:01:07.201 --> 00:01:08.850
How did you get going
in this whole area

00:01:08.851 --> 00:01:12.055
because I knew you
had a history in

00:01:12.056 --> 00:01:15.900
Nokia being in Finland
and working as

00:01:15.901 --> 00:01:17.190
a young student in

00:01:17.191 --> 00:01:19.180
areas around probability
and statistics?

00:01:19.181 --> 00:01:20.885
How did you get into this field?

00:01:20.886 --> 00:01:22.470
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I actually
started studying

00:01:22.471 --> 00:01:24.825
management science and
industrial engineering,

00:01:24.826 --> 00:01:28.770
and then just operations
research and systems analysis,

00:01:28.771 --> 00:01:33.525
and AI really just drew me
in as a undergrad really.

00:01:33.526 --> 00:01:35.110
Then, for a master's thesis,

00:01:35.111 --> 00:01:36.650
I built the Traconet,

00:01:36.651 --> 00:01:39.510
which is the first
distributed system

00:01:39.511 --> 00:01:42.070
for negotiation among
self interested agents.

00:01:42.071 --> 00:01:44.765
That really spawned
more questions than answers

00:01:44.766 --> 00:01:47.590
so I decided to come to
the US to do my PhD in that,

00:01:47.591 --> 00:01:49.950
and it's always been kind
of interdisciplinary

00:01:49.951 --> 00:01:52.715
between computer science
and in AI in particular,

00:01:52.716 --> 00:01:54.990
and operations research,
and economics,

00:01:54.991 --> 00:01:56.180
in particular game theory.

00:01:56.181 --> 00:01:57.940
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so go back to
that project thing and

00:01:57.941 --> 00:01:59.710
what was it called again and?

00:01:59.711 --> 00:02:02.690
&gt;&gt; Traconet or Transportation
Corporation Net.

00:02:02.691 --> 00:02:04.190
&gt;&gt; What was it all about?

00:02:04.191 --> 00:02:07.935
&gt;&gt; It was this thing
where I got to

00:02:07.936 --> 00:02:12.035
really shape it myself as
a master's thesis student.

00:02:12.036 --> 00:02:13.280
They have sold this project.

00:02:13.281 --> 00:02:14.155
&gt;&gt; This is in Finland?

00:02:14.156 --> 00:02:15.180
&gt;&gt; In Finland at the Technical

00:02:15.181 --> 00:02:16.680
Research Center of Finland,

00:02:16.681 --> 00:02:19.260
and they have sold
this project before I

00:02:19.261 --> 00:02:22.705
joined to some companies that
has some trucking needs.

00:02:22.706 --> 00:02:25.650
Some of them were
transporting heavy goods like

00:02:25.651 --> 00:02:27.450
construction things and others

00:02:27.451 --> 00:02:30.525
were transporting
very light things, and so on.

00:02:30.526 --> 00:02:32.370
Then, they realized that they had

00:02:32.371 --> 00:02:34.505
some potential for cooperation,

00:02:34.506 --> 00:02:37.730
reallocating tasks among
themselves, and so on.

00:02:37.731 --> 00:02:39.420
From there I could just

00:02:39.421 --> 00:02:40.920
take it in any
direction I wanted,

00:02:40.921 --> 00:02:42.980
and I wanted to build
an automated negotiations.

00:02:42.981 --> 00:02:45.430
&gt;&gt; But how did you yourself
get pulled into this?

00:02:45.431 --> 00:02:47.190
&gt;&gt; Well, I had like
three different options

00:02:47.191 --> 00:02:48.510
to look at my master's thesis

00:02:48.511 --> 00:02:51.970
and I liked that as
the most open ended novel.

00:02:51.971 --> 00:02:53.600
&gt;&gt; With these practical projects,

00:02:53.601 --> 00:02:55.235
you have to engage
in it at times?

00:02:55.236 --> 00:02:58.300
&gt;&gt; Yeah. There's
a master's thesis

00:02:58.301 --> 00:03:00.815
or master's research thesis.

00:03:00.816 --> 00:03:02.490
Yeah, and there
were many different

00:03:02.491 --> 00:03:03.800
options and I like three of them,

00:03:03.801 --> 00:03:05.000
and this I liked the best.

00:03:05.001 --> 00:03:06.900
&gt;&gt; So, what got you excited about

00:03:06.901 --> 00:03:09.010
the self interested
distributed aspects of things.

00:03:09.011 --> 00:03:10.335
&gt;&gt; Well, I had been studying

00:03:10.336 --> 00:03:12.240
game theory and optimization,

00:03:12.241 --> 00:03:13.950
and I felt that a lot

00:03:13.951 --> 00:03:15.640
of the stuff hadn't
been put together.

00:03:15.641 --> 00:03:17.040
So, there's a lot of good work.

00:03:17.041 --> 00:03:18.770
I needed to programming
and things like that,

00:03:18.771 --> 00:03:20.375
and a lot of good
work on game theory.

00:03:20.376 --> 00:03:24.010
People hadn't really put
those two things together.

00:03:24.011 --> 00:03:26.580
&gt;&gt; Did this trucking
system work out?

00:03:26.581 --> 00:03:28.920
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, it was

00:03:28.921 --> 00:03:31.045
just a prototype system so we

00:03:31.046 --> 00:03:32.160
collected real data and

00:03:32.161 --> 00:03:33.660
showed how it would
work on real data,

00:03:33.661 --> 00:03:35.070
but it wasn't ever, we

00:03:35.071 --> 00:03:36.560
didn't actually field
it or anything.

00:03:36.561 --> 00:03:38.680
That wasn't even the goal. It
was really just a prototype

00:03:38.681 --> 00:03:41.565
and then I left to
the US to do my PhD.

00:03:41.566 --> 00:03:46.375
&gt;&gt; Okay. Now, before we get
into the PhD work and beyond,

00:03:46.376 --> 00:03:48.780
let's take a brief break

00:03:48.781 --> 00:03:51.825
and talk about your
windsurfing career.

00:03:51.826 --> 00:03:54.060
So, how did that get
going and how did you

00:03:54.061 --> 00:03:56.400
become the Finland champion
in windsurfing?

00:03:56.401 --> 00:03:57.680
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, I've

00:03:57.681 --> 00:04:00.920
been racing sailor boats
since I was a little kid.

00:04:00.921 --> 00:04:04.180
Then, you can really sail

00:04:04.181 --> 00:04:06.000
optimist thing until
you're 15 but I

00:04:06.001 --> 00:04:08.080
got too big and heavy so
the boat wouldn't just go.

00:04:08.081 --> 00:04:10.650
So, I needed a different class

00:04:10.651 --> 00:04:13.495
and windsurfing had just
become so hot and athletic,

00:04:13.496 --> 00:04:15.840
and it could actually take
a little bit of a bigger guy.

00:04:15.841 --> 00:04:19.580
So, I started windsurfing and
then in a couple of years,

00:04:19.581 --> 00:04:21.060
some of the coaches in

00:04:21.061 --> 00:04:25.160
Finland saw me and how
I did in the races,

00:04:25.161 --> 00:04:26.730
and they picked me
onto the national team

00:04:26.731 --> 00:04:27.970
for four and a half years.

00:04:27.971 --> 00:04:31.025
So, throughout high school
and throughout the air force,

00:04:31.026 --> 00:04:34.860
I was actually sailing around
in the world championships.

00:04:34.861 --> 00:04:36.960
So, when I'm windsurfing
and in the winters,

00:04:36.961 --> 00:04:38.340
we would go down south to

00:04:38.341 --> 00:04:40.640
practice when Finland was
all covered with ice.

00:04:40.641 --> 00:04:42.670
&gt;&gt; Down south where?

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:46.995
&gt;&gt; Canary Islands.
Sometimes, Spain.

00:04:46.996 --> 00:04:48.465
&gt;&gt; Where does
a Finnish person go to

00:04:48.466 --> 00:04:50.570
practice windsurfing
in the summer?

00:04:50.571 --> 00:04:50.772
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:04:50.773 --> 00:04:53.360
&gt;&gt; It's interesting. Yeah, I was

00:04:53.361 --> 00:04:54.660
impressed that you
mention this in

00:04:54.661 --> 00:04:56.795
the past and I even
see in your formal CV.

00:04:56.796 --> 00:04:57.140
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:04:57.141 --> 00:04:58.505
&gt;&gt; I see '86 of
this as we're going

00:04:58.506 --> 00:05:00.750
Finland windsurfing champ
for the year.

00:05:00.751 --> 00:05:03.660
&gt;&gt; Yes, and my best race

00:05:03.661 --> 00:05:05.910
was actually being the
fifth in the Europeans.

00:05:05.911 --> 00:05:07.220
I was also 12th in the world.

00:05:07.221 --> 00:05:09.825
But really, the best races
were in the Europeans.

00:05:09.826 --> 00:05:11.635
&gt;&gt; Before we get off that do
you still do windsurfing?

00:05:11.636 --> 00:05:12.470
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:05:12.471 --> 00:05:14.225
&gt;&gt; Even though it's
not hot any more.

00:05:14.226 --> 00:05:15.670
&gt;&gt; No, it's actually

00:05:15.671 --> 00:05:17.305
really nice that it's
not hot anymore.

00:05:17.306 --> 00:05:18.970
You have a lot more
space to do it.

00:05:18.971 --> 00:05:21.140
I thought you'd be on
paddle-boards by this time.

00:05:21.141 --> 00:05:25.090
&gt;&gt; No, no. So, I'm
loyal to what I do.

00:05:25.091 --> 00:05:27.100
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and never ice hockey.

00:05:27.101 --> 00:05:29.085
&gt;&gt; I played ice hockey as a kid.

00:05:29.086 --> 00:05:30.190
&gt;&gt; Okay. Just sometime-

00:05:30.191 --> 00:05:31.510
&gt;&gt; Being a Finnish kid,
you don't have to.

00:05:31.511 --> 00:05:32.610
&gt;&gt; Just checking in on this.

00:05:32.611 --> 00:05:33.370
&gt;&gt; Yes, I know you play.

00:05:33.371 --> 00:05:35.710
&gt;&gt; So, then you went
off to Amherst.

00:05:35.711 --> 00:05:36.070
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:05:36.071 --> 00:05:37.570
&gt;&gt; How was that experience?

00:05:37.571 --> 00:05:38.980
What kind of things
to work on there?

00:05:38.981 --> 00:05:42.610
&gt;&gt; Great. I had read
a lot of papers in

00:05:42.611 --> 00:05:44.090
multi-agent systems already doing

00:05:44.091 --> 00:05:47.215
this transportation corporation
net system in Finland,

00:05:47.216 --> 00:05:48.770
and I looked at who's

00:05:48.771 --> 00:05:50.470
publishing most in
multi-agent system,

00:05:50.471 --> 00:05:51.960
and I sent Victor

00:05:51.961 --> 00:05:54.115
lesser email and he accepted
me as a grad student.

00:05:54.116 --> 00:05:57.460
We actually flew there to
meet before we committed.

00:05:57.461 --> 00:05:59.510
So, we interviewed each
other a little bit,

00:05:59.511 --> 00:06:02.085
and then we decided to work
together and it was great.

00:06:02.086 --> 00:06:05.855
&gt;&gt; I always found Victor
with many years as a very,

00:06:05.856 --> 00:06:08.655
very kind, thoughtful,
caring man.

00:06:08.656 --> 00:06:09.700
&gt;&gt; That's right. We still talk

00:06:09.701 --> 00:06:11.965
about stuff and he's
still my mentor.

00:06:11.966 --> 00:06:14.645
&gt;&gt; That's fabulous. I'm
sure that he probably,

00:06:14.646 --> 00:06:17.810
some of his personality,

00:06:17.811 --> 00:06:19.420
his mentorship abilities have

00:06:19.421 --> 00:06:20.810
rubbed off on you over the years.

00:06:20.811 --> 00:06:21.970
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I try.

00:06:21.971 --> 00:06:24.240
&gt;&gt; So, you finished that up

00:06:24.241 --> 00:06:26.360
and then what was next
for you, your PhD?

00:06:26.361 --> 00:06:28.180
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, then I went to be

00:06:28.181 --> 00:06:30.640
assistant professor at
Washington University,

00:06:30.641 --> 00:06:33.870
St. Louis, where I started
my own research group.

00:06:33.871 --> 00:06:36.405
Then, four years later,

00:06:36.406 --> 00:06:38.665
CMU hired me out of there.

00:06:38.666 --> 00:06:41.430
&gt;&gt; So, at CMU, you've
been this force

00:06:41.431 --> 00:06:45.160
in combinatorial auctions and

00:06:45.161 --> 00:06:46.780
some interesting results
you've proved them,

00:06:46.781 --> 00:06:47.980
and you and your students have

00:06:47.981 --> 00:06:50.660
proved in that space
over the years.

00:06:50.661 --> 00:06:55.175
You've not only done
this great scholarship,

00:06:55.176 --> 00:06:58.890
you've been spinning out
companies like CombineNet.

00:06:58.891 --> 00:07:01.540
I think you were running
CombineNet for many years.

00:07:01.541 --> 00:07:03.095
It must have been
at least a decade.

00:07:03.096 --> 00:07:03.750
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:07:03.751 --> 00:07:05.070
&gt;&gt; So, what was that all about?

00:07:05.071 --> 00:07:06.960
The CombineNet experience?

00:07:06.961 --> 00:07:09.160
&gt;&gt; Yes, CombineNet, I started

00:07:09.161 --> 00:07:11.054
building the technology in '97,

00:07:11.055 --> 00:07:15.920
and then I founded the company
in 2000. You're right.

00:07:15.921 --> 00:07:17.740
I mean, we were continuing until

00:07:17.741 --> 00:07:19.750
2010 before it was acquired,

00:07:19.751 --> 00:07:21.790
and we fielded 800 of

00:07:21.791 --> 00:07:23.410
the world's still largest scale

00:07:23.411 --> 00:07:25.465
combinatorial auctions
ever fielded,

00:07:25.466 --> 00:07:27.820
totaling $60 billion
and we created

00:07:27.821 --> 00:07:30.555
a supply chain efficiency
of over $6 billion.

00:07:30.556 --> 00:07:31.990
&gt;&gt; So, then of
course, they gave you

00:07:31.991 --> 00:07:34.050
most of that money, right?

00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:37.385
&gt;&gt; Sourcing software
is the worst business,

00:07:37.386 --> 00:07:41.000
I can tell that, in
terms of raw margins.

00:07:41.001 --> 00:07:43.420
&gt;&gt; Well, hopefully a little
piece of it at some point.

00:07:43.421 --> 00:07:45.670
&gt;&gt; Yeah, you have to
basically sell software

00:07:45.671 --> 00:07:48.330
to the toughest
negotiators in the world.

00:07:48.331 --> 00:07:50.665
That's what they do all day long.

00:07:50.666 --> 00:07:52.390
They push their suppliers down on

00:07:52.391 --> 00:07:54.490
pricing so they do the same
to the software vendors.

00:07:54.491 --> 00:07:55.800
&gt;&gt; So, what was
your experience like with

00:07:55.801 --> 00:07:58.390
the whole startup world?

00:07:58.391 --> 00:08:00.025
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it was very interesting.

00:08:00.026 --> 00:08:05.020
A lot of people think
that the interaction

00:08:05.021 --> 00:08:07.150
between academia
and startup is like

00:08:07.151 --> 00:08:09.800
you just license
technology from academia,

00:08:09.801 --> 00:08:13.210
and it's a one way, but
this was really both ways.

00:08:13.211 --> 00:08:15.940
Actually, more so the other way,

00:08:15.941 --> 00:08:19.465
where we actually saw
problems in the real world,

00:08:19.466 --> 00:08:21.695
that hadn't been
looked at in academia.

00:08:21.696 --> 00:08:23.140
Many of those things, we actually

00:08:23.141 --> 00:08:24.610
brought back to my academic lab,

00:08:24.611 --> 00:08:27.060
and then we were the first ones
to look at these things,

00:08:27.061 --> 00:08:30.635
like, preference elicitation
in multi-agent systems,

00:08:30.636 --> 00:08:37.270
automated mechanism design,
structured domain

00:08:37.271 --> 00:08:38.633
specific building languages

00:08:38.634 --> 00:08:42.421
for combinatorial auctions etc.

00:08:42.422 --> 00:08:44.610
&gt;&gt; So, when did

00:08:44.611 --> 00:08:50.790
you start getting involved
in code from that realm?

00:08:50.791 --> 00:08:53.140
I see the relationships
but some people in

00:08:53.141 --> 00:08:56.160
the outside world might
not see them to the world,

00:08:56.161 --> 00:08:57.590
the realm of kidney exchanges.

00:08:57.591 --> 00:08:58.750
How did you get exposed to that?

00:08:58.751 --> 00:09:00.625
Maybe you can explain to folks,

00:09:00.626 --> 00:09:03.730
some of the things with
the big grand challenge of

00:09:03.731 --> 00:09:07.410
kidney exchanges is and
why it's a hard problem,

00:09:07.411 --> 00:09:09.610
and why it's so
valuable to solve,

00:09:09.611 --> 00:09:11.550
and then how you get
interested in that?

00:09:11.551 --> 00:09:12.660
Well, it is a multi-part
question there.

00:09:12.661 --> 00:09:14.240
&gt;&gt; Okay great. So, how

00:09:14.241 --> 00:09:15.680
I got interested in
kidney exchanges-

00:09:15.681 --> 00:09:17.270
&gt;&gt; What is it first of all?
What is this? Because people

00:09:17.271 --> 00:09:19.740
don't have any understanding
at all about this.

00:09:19.741 --> 00:09:22.765
&gt;&gt; Okay. So,
kidney exchange is there

00:09:22.766 --> 00:09:25.395
to help alleviate the problem

00:09:25.396 --> 00:09:27.955
if there aren't
enough organ donors,

00:09:27.956 --> 00:09:29.930
who donate organs when they die.

00:09:29.931 --> 00:09:32.815
So, there's this big imbalance
of supply and demand.

00:09:32.816 --> 00:09:35.300
Not just in the US,
but in most countries.

00:09:35.301 --> 00:09:37.165
So, for example, in the US,

00:09:37.166 --> 00:09:39.580
there are over 100,000
people that are listed to

00:09:39.581 --> 00:09:42.810
wait for dead people's kidneys,

00:09:42.811 --> 00:09:44.920
and these are people
who are very sick,

00:09:44.921 --> 00:09:48.080
and are eventually going to
die if they don't get them.

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:50.980
So, you can do live

00:09:50.981 --> 00:09:52.970
donation because most people
have two kidneys.

00:09:52.971 --> 00:09:54.465
Like if you get sick,

00:09:54.466 --> 00:09:56.125
I can give you one of my kidneys.

00:09:56.126 --> 00:09:57.585
I offered to give you one.

00:09:57.586 --> 00:10:00.200
But chances are, I
can't due to blood type

00:10:00.201 --> 00:10:03.095
and tissue type incompatibility
and few other factors.

00:10:03.096 --> 00:10:05.420
So, that's where
kidney exchange comes in.

00:10:05.421 --> 00:10:07.870
So, you actually,
at the simplest,

00:10:07.871 --> 00:10:09.165
to swap the donors.

00:10:09.166 --> 00:10:11.725
So, if we were a willing pair,

00:10:11.726 --> 00:10:13.110
donor patient pair,

00:10:13.111 --> 00:10:15.600
some other pair is willing
but also incompatible.

00:10:15.601 --> 00:10:16.855
&gt;&gt; It could be
anywhere in the world?

00:10:16.856 --> 00:10:19.665
&gt;&gt; Well, currently, they're
just willing countries,

00:10:19.666 --> 00:10:21.230
but many countries have them.

00:10:21.231 --> 00:10:23.030
But so think about in the US,

00:10:23.031 --> 00:10:25.040
California and Massachusetts say.

00:10:25.041 --> 00:10:27.280
So, I am going to
donate to somebody

00:10:27.281 --> 00:10:29.710
else and their donor is
going to donate to you.

00:10:29.711 --> 00:10:31.470
Now both of the
patients can live.

00:10:31.471 --> 00:10:32.810
If you think about that from

00:10:32.811 --> 00:10:35.500
a graph perspective,
that is a two cycle.

00:10:35.501 --> 00:10:38.700
You can also do three cycles
and you can do what we

00:10:38.701 --> 00:10:42.770
call never ending
arteries donor initiated chains.

00:10:42.771 --> 00:10:44.870
So, somebody actually
gives a kidney,

00:10:44.871 --> 00:10:46.850
does not expect
anything in return.

00:10:46.851 --> 00:10:48.170
Instead of a cycle,

00:10:48.171 --> 00:10:50.105
that triggers a chain
in the network,

00:10:50.106 --> 00:10:53.300
and at the end of the chain
there is last willing donor,

00:10:53.301 --> 00:10:55.780
which can serve as
this outreach in

00:10:55.781 --> 00:10:57.525
the next week's
optimization patch

00:10:57.526 --> 00:10:59.230
and it is a gift
that keeps giving.

00:10:59.231 --> 00:11:02.950
But the hard optimization
problem in the batch setting is

00:11:02.951 --> 00:11:05.215
figuring out
the optimal combination

00:11:05.216 --> 00:11:07.095
of cycles and shapes.

00:11:07.096 --> 00:11:08.705
&gt;&gt; Okay, so that
is the challenge.

00:11:08.706 --> 00:11:09.940
How on earth did you first get

00:11:09.941 --> 00:11:11.845
exposed to this and
get excited about it?

00:11:11.846 --> 00:11:14.840
&gt;&gt; So, I was on the
organizational committee of

00:11:14.841 --> 00:11:16.270
the International Congress of the

00:11:16.271 --> 00:11:18.619
Game Theory Society in 2004,

00:11:18.620 --> 00:11:22.095
and as one of our invited
speakers we invited Al Roffe,

00:11:22.096 --> 00:11:23.745
who had just started

00:11:23.746 --> 00:11:26.635
working with some
medical collaborators

00:11:26.636 --> 00:11:28.205
and some other economists

00:11:28.206 --> 00:11:31.840
on the New England
Paired Kidney Exchange.

00:11:31.841 --> 00:11:33.660
He spoke about kidney exchange,

00:11:33.661 --> 00:11:35.445
so that is how I
first heard about it

00:11:35.446 --> 00:11:37.990
and I saw that there were
a lot of computer science,

00:11:37.991 --> 00:11:40.585
the ORS' problems in
there and I thought

00:11:40.586 --> 00:11:43.895
that that would be a nice way
to do some pro bono work.

00:11:43.896 --> 00:11:45.640
&gt;&gt; And to give back.

00:11:45.641 --> 00:11:47.485
&gt;&gt; And to give back.
Yeah, exactly.

00:11:47.486 --> 00:11:49.105
So this is not a start up.

00:11:49.106 --> 00:11:52.450
We run the nationwide
kidney exchange for UNOS,

00:11:52.451 --> 00:11:54.870
so we donate the software
for my [inaudible]

00:11:54.871 --> 00:11:56.320
&gt;&gt; What is UNOS?

00:11:56.321 --> 00:11:59.320
&gt;&gt; UNOS Is the United Network
for Organ Sharing.

00:11:59.321 --> 00:12:02.060
They control the transplant
patients in the US,

00:12:02.061 --> 00:12:04.470
both from the deceased
donor side and

00:12:04.471 --> 00:12:07.260
kidney exchange side at

00:12:07.261 --> 00:12:09.305
least for the National
Kidney Exchange,

00:12:09.306 --> 00:12:13.080
and our software runs
the kidney exchange side of it.

00:12:13.081 --> 00:12:15.300
So every week it makes
a transplant plan

00:12:15.301 --> 00:12:17.530
for 69% of the
transplant centers.

00:12:17.531 --> 00:12:17.925
&gt;&gt; That is amazing.

00:12:17.926 --> 00:12:19.740
&gt;&gt; In the US. This year

00:12:19.741 --> 00:12:23.350
we are we giving away the
software. It is not a startup.

00:12:23.351 --> 00:12:25.850
&gt;&gt; Well, first of all
before I even get to how it

00:12:25.851 --> 00:12:28.120
works and how you
monitor it and so on,

00:12:28.121 --> 00:12:31.425
what hard core problems

00:12:31.426 --> 00:12:34.410
even if they are just
applications and transfer of

00:12:34.411 --> 00:12:36.250
existing ideas did you have

00:12:36.251 --> 00:12:41.160
to work with to solve
the kidney exchange challenge?

00:12:41.161 --> 00:12:44.550
&gt;&gt; So, one was just this
optimization problem

00:12:44.551 --> 00:12:46.240
and the batch
optimization problem.

00:12:46.241 --> 00:12:48.290
But then as we got more into it,

00:12:48.291 --> 00:12:50.950
we realized that that
is not the only thing.

00:12:50.951 --> 00:12:53.010
There are a lot of things
that go around it.

00:12:53.011 --> 00:12:56.880
So, for example the problem
is dynamic; patients die,

00:12:56.881 --> 00:12:58.865
they get deceased donor organs,

00:12:58.866 --> 00:13:00.915
other kidney exchanges
may snipe them,

00:13:00.916 --> 00:13:02.210
other things might happen,

00:13:02.211 --> 00:13:04.075
they get sick, they
cannot be transplanted.

00:13:04.076 --> 00:13:05.790
So, the problem is dynamic and

00:13:05.791 --> 00:13:07.560
also new pairs come
to the exchange,

00:13:07.561 --> 00:13:10.100
new outreach come to
the exchange and so on.

00:13:10.101 --> 00:13:12.540
Another issue is that

00:13:12.541 --> 00:13:15.250
there are what I call
pre-transplant failures.

00:13:15.251 --> 00:13:17.660
So, the medical
knowledge is incomplete.

00:13:17.661 --> 00:13:20.235
If it says that we
are compatible,

00:13:20.236 --> 00:13:22.435
in reality we may or may not be.

00:13:22.436 --> 00:13:26.310
It turns out that
most kidney exchange structures,

00:13:26.311 --> 00:13:28.595
cycles of chains worldwide

00:13:28.596 --> 00:13:31.275
actually fail before
transplant because if

00:13:31.276 --> 00:13:34.400
even one of those edges
turns out to be actually not

00:13:34.401 --> 00:13:36.160
compatible or one of

00:13:36.161 --> 00:13:38.790
the patients dies or gets
a deceased donor organ,

00:13:38.791 --> 00:13:39.870
something happens between

00:13:39.871 --> 00:13:42.260
the planning stage
and the operation,

00:13:42.261 --> 00:13:44.495
which is about eight
weeks typically,

00:13:44.496 --> 00:13:46.355
the whole structure fails,

00:13:46.356 --> 00:13:49.005
90% of the structures fail.

00:13:49.006 --> 00:13:52.280
So, how can we actually do
this failure aware planning.

00:13:52.281 --> 00:13:53.360
We just had a paper out in

00:13:53.361 --> 00:13:57.450
management science on
that and you can get

00:13:57.451 --> 00:14:00.955
much better numbers
of transplants

00:14:00.956 --> 00:14:05.060
by not just looking at how
good an edge would be,

00:14:05.061 --> 00:14:06.300
how good it would be,

00:14:06.301 --> 00:14:08.690
but how likely it is
to succeed as well.

00:14:08.691 --> 00:14:10.820
So, now you are taking
extra information into

00:14:10.821 --> 00:14:13.125
account and you are making
these more robust plans.

00:14:13.126 --> 00:14:15.380
&gt;&gt; What is it likely
to come from?

00:14:15.381 --> 00:14:16.010
&gt;&gt; The likelihood.

00:14:16.011 --> 00:14:16.840
&gt;&gt; Of succeeding, I mean.

00:14:16.841 --> 00:14:18.400
&gt;&gt; The likelihood of

00:14:18.401 --> 00:14:21.180
succeeding could come from
machine learning models,

00:14:21.181 --> 00:14:23.190
but even if you
just say that it is

00:14:23.191 --> 00:14:26.570
the same average probability
across everybody,

00:14:26.571 --> 00:14:30.840
you get still 100-150% lift
in transplant.

00:14:30.841 --> 00:14:33.360
So, even if you do not take
into account the fact that

00:14:33.361 --> 00:14:35.750
some ages are more likely
to fail than others.

00:14:35.751 --> 00:14:37.840
And what this looks like then in

00:14:37.841 --> 00:14:40.680
practice you have shorter cycles,

00:14:40.681 --> 00:14:42.520
you have shorter chains and

00:14:42.521 --> 00:14:44.950
more parallelism in the plans.

00:14:44.951 --> 00:14:47.360
So then those structures are less

00:14:47.361 --> 00:14:49.860
likely to fail because
they have fewer edges,

00:14:49.861 --> 00:14:53.520
and if they do fail it
affects fewer transplants.

00:14:53.521 --> 00:14:54.740
&gt;&gt; I see.

00:14:54.741 --> 00:15:00.520
Is it the case that the patient

00:15:00.521 --> 00:15:02.970
and nephrologist
and team and family

00:15:02.971 --> 00:15:06.840
will ever have to do
this idea of a trade,

00:15:06.841 --> 00:15:10.010
like, my kidney function

00:15:10.011 --> 00:15:13.360
is crashing at this rate or
I can't do more dialysis.

00:15:13.361 --> 00:15:15.980
It's hard to wait longer
but this match is not

00:15:15.981 --> 00:15:18.700
great to wait for
the next match and

00:15:18.701 --> 00:15:21.010
have a probability distribution
over the problem of

00:15:21.011 --> 00:15:24.345
getting a better match
within that time horizon?

00:15:24.346 --> 00:15:26.020
&gt;&gt; Yes. Some people
have looked at

00:15:26.021 --> 00:15:30.995
that as a decision as to
how long should you wait.

00:15:30.996 --> 00:15:32.590
We haven't really studied that so

00:15:32.591 --> 00:15:34.465
much and it is my understanding

00:15:34.466 --> 00:15:37.570
that patients really are not

00:15:37.571 --> 00:15:40.960
presented a menu of kidneys
or anything like that.

00:15:40.961 --> 00:15:44.265
It is more like when
a matching kidneys found,

00:15:44.266 --> 00:15:46.100
people are very happy with it.

00:15:46.101 --> 00:15:48.590
Now, I will tell you a little bit

00:15:48.591 --> 00:15:50.420
of a counterexample
to what I just said.

00:15:50.421 --> 00:15:54.030
We were working with the
Alliance for Paired Donation.

00:15:54.031 --> 00:15:56.590
This was in 2006-2007.

00:15:56.591 --> 00:16:01.555
It is a private kidney
exchange and there,

00:16:01.556 --> 00:16:03.320
there was this, I want to say

00:16:03.321 --> 00:16:06.675
60-year-old uncle and
a 13-year-old kid,

00:16:06.676 --> 00:16:09.395
this 13-year-old-girl, and it is

00:16:09.396 --> 00:16:12.740
clear that that
60-year-old kidney would

00:16:12.741 --> 00:16:15.600
not last adequately long in

00:16:15.601 --> 00:16:16.630
that little kid because

00:16:16.631 --> 00:16:18.575
the little kid still had
a lot of life ahead of her.

00:16:18.576 --> 00:16:20.060
&gt;&gt; This thing is to look
at the actual amount

00:16:20.061 --> 00:16:21.500
of trade you have
on the RP receive

00:16:21.501 --> 00:16:24.495
into a patient whose tread
is there for the future.

00:16:24.496 --> 00:16:26.360
&gt;&gt; So they were actually

00:16:26.361 --> 00:16:28.815
compatible already without
the kidney exchange,

00:16:28.816 --> 00:16:30.820
but they came to
the kidney exchange and said

00:16:30.821 --> 00:16:33.185
that you have three months,

00:16:33.186 --> 00:16:36.420
if you can find
a better match for the girl,

00:16:36.421 --> 00:16:38.665
the uncle is going to
give to somebody else,

00:16:38.666 --> 00:16:40.440
and we actually
algorithmically found

00:16:40.441 --> 00:16:42.585
a better match just in
the normal process.

00:16:42.586 --> 00:16:45.410
So, now we took
this compatible pair,

00:16:45.411 --> 00:16:47.805
put them into
the kidney exchange,

00:16:47.806 --> 00:16:51.140
and for somebody who was older,

00:16:51.141 --> 00:16:54.765
that uncle's kidney was great.

00:16:54.766 --> 00:16:57.020
Then, the girl got
a younger kidney.

00:16:57.021 --> 00:16:58.495
&gt;&gt; I hadn't really
thought about in

00:16:58.496 --> 00:17:00.540
organ transplants that you

00:17:00.541 --> 00:17:02.640
have the age of the tissue itself

00:17:02.641 --> 00:17:04.070
and has piece of little, where.

00:17:04.071 --> 00:17:05.370
Will these cells live as long as

00:17:05.371 --> 00:17:07.450
this patient perform over
the time when they have

00:17:07.451 --> 00:17:11.795
their own lifespan as part
of the donor's life history?

00:17:11.796 --> 00:17:12.320
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:17:12.321 --> 00:17:15.620
&gt;&gt; Very interesting.
So, moving on from

00:17:15.621 --> 00:17:20.360
the medical successes
into other areas.

00:17:21.090 --> 00:17:26.090
So, you see your team
is renowned for beating

00:17:26.091 --> 00:17:28.380
the best players at

00:17:28.381 --> 00:17:32.210
heads of no limit
Texas Hold'em poker.

00:17:32.211 --> 00:17:37.690
This is remarkable in
some ways in that it goes

00:17:37.691 --> 00:17:41.090
beyond what we often refer to as

00:17:41.091 --> 00:17:45.275
the simplicity frankly of
games of perfect information.

00:17:45.276 --> 00:17:49.660
We could explain the difference
going from checkers or

00:17:49.661 --> 00:17:55.664
Go into the realm of
uncertainty bluffing-

00:17:55.665 --> 00:17:56.020
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:17:56.021 --> 00:17:57.395
&gt;&gt; -human dimension.

00:17:57.396 --> 00:18:02.400
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, imperfect
information games like let's

00:18:02.401 --> 00:18:07.480
say tic tac toe,
checkers, chess, Go.

00:18:07.481 --> 00:18:09.630
You know exactly what
the state of the world

00:18:09.631 --> 00:18:12.325
is when it's your turn to move.

00:18:12.326 --> 00:18:15.450
Then, you can use techniques
like Minimax search,

00:18:15.451 --> 00:18:16.570
Alpha-beta pruning,

00:18:16.571 --> 00:18:19.765
and end-game databases
that all stuff.

00:18:19.766 --> 00:18:22.275
&gt;&gt; You can build
huge databases by just

00:18:22.276 --> 00:18:24.715
playing oneself
a trillion times as well.

00:18:24.716 --> 00:18:26.700
&gt;&gt; Yes. There have
been great successes.

00:18:26.701 --> 00:18:28.910
Some of those games are
very difficult as well.

00:18:28.911 --> 00:18:30.845
Like chess is still unsolved,

00:18:30.846 --> 00:18:33.300
Go still unsolved, but they

00:18:33.301 --> 00:18:34.510
have reached superhuman level

00:18:34.511 --> 00:18:36.065
at that and it's very impressive.

00:18:36.066 --> 00:18:37.830
In imperfect information game,

00:18:37.831 --> 00:18:39.950
the situation is
totally different.

00:18:39.951 --> 00:18:41.990
None of those
algorithms is applied.

00:18:41.991 --> 00:18:44.580
In imperfect information games,

00:18:44.581 --> 00:18:45.930
you don't know the state of

00:18:45.931 --> 00:18:47.725
the world when it's
your turn to act.

00:18:47.726 --> 00:18:49.310
To complicate things,

00:18:49.311 --> 00:18:51.090
your opponents may know things

00:18:51.091 --> 00:18:53.015
that you don't know
and vice versa.

00:18:53.016 --> 00:18:55.680
So, you have to
think about what do

00:18:55.681 --> 00:18:56.960
my actions signal to

00:18:56.961 --> 00:18:59.340
the opponent about
my private information?

00:18:59.341 --> 00:19:00.600
I have to think about what do

00:19:00.601 --> 00:19:01.950
my opponent's actions signal

00:19:01.951 --> 00:19:03.860
to me about their
private information?

00:19:03.861 --> 00:19:05.330
It is balancing act.

00:19:05.331 --> 00:19:07.070
&gt;&gt; The history of
the evidential stream as well.

00:19:07.071 --> 00:19:08.690
&gt;&gt; Exactly. Well, absolutely.

00:19:08.691 --> 00:19:10.980
You have to think about
the history of the actions

00:19:10.981 --> 00:19:13.480
because that's where
the beliefs get updated from.

00:19:13.481 --> 00:19:14.000
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:19:14.001 --> 00:19:16.250
&gt;&gt; So, you would think about

00:19:16.251 --> 00:19:18.620
this as a generalization
of [inaudible] piece

00:19:18.621 --> 00:19:21.030
brought into the game setting.

00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:24.340
So, it's much more difficult

00:19:24.341 --> 00:19:25.420
because you have to think about

00:19:25.421 --> 00:19:26.980
those beliefs and then you

00:19:26.981 --> 00:19:28.915
need to go to game
theoretic reasoning.

00:19:28.916 --> 00:19:30.400
So, it's actually interesting

00:19:30.401 --> 00:19:31.980
that there how you should update

00:19:31.981 --> 00:19:34.870
the beliefs falls out of

00:19:34.871 --> 00:19:36.890
the classic game
theory solution concepts

00:19:36.891 --> 00:19:39.210
like Nash equilibrium
and it's refinements.

00:19:39.211 --> 00:19:42.200
But the works really
about how do you marry

00:19:42.201 --> 00:19:45.875
those concepts with
algorithms from AI,

00:19:45.876 --> 00:19:47.625
so we can actually compute

00:19:47.626 --> 00:19:48.880
good approximations of

00:19:48.881 --> 00:19:50.890
a Nash equilibrium
and it's refinements.

00:19:50.891 --> 00:19:52.270
&gt;&gt; Okay, so people
have been trying to

00:19:52.271 --> 00:19:54.820
solve this problem for decades.

00:19:54.821 --> 00:19:57.160
What was the magic or

00:19:57.161 --> 00:20:00.510
the unique innovations that
your team came up with?

00:20:00.511 --> 00:20:01.860
I guess I have to say it was

00:20:01.861 --> 00:20:04.090
an one cycle you've been
doing this for a while now-

00:20:04.091 --> 00:20:04.480
&gt;&gt; That's right

00:20:04.481 --> 00:20:06.460
&gt;&gt; -and you finally
got the win. But what

00:20:06.461 --> 00:20:08.875
were some of
the findings in results.

00:20:08.876 --> 00:20:12.520
&gt;&gt; Okay, so there
have been a lot.

00:20:12.521 --> 00:20:15.490
So, I've been working on Poker

00:20:15.491 --> 00:20:18.950
as one of the
benchmarks since 2004.

00:20:18.951 --> 00:20:22.440
So, from 2004 to 2017, 13 years.

00:20:22.441 --> 00:20:24.310
So, it wasn't like one night

00:20:24.311 --> 00:20:25.980
we came up with something
and it was done.

00:20:25.981 --> 00:20:29.120
It was just like
accumulation of ideas.

00:20:29.121 --> 00:20:31.460
&gt;&gt; I can see this is
the same time span.

00:20:31.461 --> 00:20:35.050
We have Thomas thinking about
kidney exchanges and then

00:20:35.051 --> 00:20:38.755
take a break.Poker
that's kidney exchange.

00:20:38.756 --> 00:20:40.600
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's maybe

00:20:40.601 --> 00:20:43.730
that's my style of research
at combinatorial auctions

00:20:43.731 --> 00:20:45.725
where you're 10 15 years
kidney exchange for

00:20:45.726 --> 00:20:46.790
10 to 15 years and still

00:20:46.791 --> 00:20:48.710
is and combinatorial
auctions too.

00:20:48.711 --> 00:20:51.020
But it takes a while to really

00:20:51.021 --> 00:20:53.540
learn the problem and learn
the techniques at work,

00:20:53.541 --> 00:20:55.770
so it's not like one paper
and that's brilliant

00:20:55.771 --> 00:20:59.095
and solves the whole thing
it doesn't work like that.

00:20:59.096 --> 00:21:02.770
Yeah. So, one is
abstraction techniques.

00:21:02.771 --> 00:21:03.940
How do you automatically

00:21:03.941 --> 00:21:06.610
abstract the game into
smaller games so that

00:21:06.611 --> 00:21:08.385
when you solve the smaller game

00:21:08.386 --> 00:21:10.790
perhaps even not
only approximately?

00:21:10.791 --> 00:21:12.770
The results are good answers.

00:21:12.771 --> 00:21:15.345
The strategies are good
strategies for the original game.

00:21:15.346 --> 00:21:18.335
The second is the equilibrium
finding algorithms.

00:21:18.336 --> 00:21:23.985
So how do you actually take
a game and find exact or

00:21:23.986 --> 00:21:26.610
near optimal strategies
for that game in

00:21:26.611 --> 00:21:28.290
a computationally efficient way

00:21:28.291 --> 00:21:30.910
both for memory and for speed?

00:21:30.911 --> 00:21:36.470
There I would say the number
one thing that actually got

00:21:36.471 --> 00:21:38.810
us over the finish line at
the very end clearly there

00:21:38.811 --> 00:21:41.260
was accumulation of
work up to then.

00:21:41.261 --> 00:21:45.405
But the last thing was these
sub game solving techniques.

00:21:45.406 --> 00:21:49.110
Safe new sub game solving
techniques that really got

00:21:49.111 --> 00:21:54.130
the final jump 63 milibig plans
per game jump over.

00:21:54.131 --> 00:21:56.935
&gt;&gt; Will say more about that,
what [inaudible] sub game plan?

00:21:56.936 --> 00:21:58.630
&gt;&gt; So normally if you-

00:21:58.631 --> 00:22:00.340
&gt;&gt; What do you do you mean
by sub game plan, also?

00:22:00.341 --> 00:22:01.730
&gt;&gt; So, if you normally think

00:22:01.731 --> 00:22:04.120
about perfect information games,

00:22:04.121 --> 00:22:07.870
you can think about let's
say talk about chess.

00:22:07.871 --> 00:22:10.295
So, you get to a state

00:22:10.296 --> 00:22:11.820
and then you start thinking

00:22:11.821 --> 00:22:13.675
about what do you do from there.

00:22:13.676 --> 00:22:15.900
You think from
there to the end of

00:22:15.901 --> 00:22:18.865
the game or to some virtual end
of the game if you will.

00:22:18.866 --> 00:22:22.900
This is easy relatively

00:22:22.901 --> 00:22:25.600
speaking in perfect
information games

00:22:25.601 --> 00:22:30.370
because you can solve

00:22:30.371 --> 00:22:32.530
the game theoretic solution for

00:22:32.531 --> 00:22:35.980
sub game based on information
only in that sub game.

00:22:35.981 --> 00:22:38.400
In imperfect information
games you can't,

00:22:38.401 --> 00:22:40.910
because you have to think
about the balance of

00:22:40.911 --> 00:22:42.260
strategies across

00:22:42.261 --> 00:22:44.880
multiple imperfect information
game solve games,

00:22:44.881 --> 00:22:46.910
so as to not

00:22:46.911 --> 00:22:49.670
give out too much information
for your opponent,

00:22:49.671 --> 00:22:54.360
and what these sub game

00:22:54.361 --> 00:22:56.460
solving techniques
do is that they

00:22:56.461 --> 00:23:00.265
take this idea of
sub game solving into

00:23:00.266 --> 00:23:02.530
imperfect information
games which is

00:23:02.531 --> 00:23:05.185
very different than in
perfect information games,

00:23:05.186 --> 00:23:07.410
and the techniques actually
look totally different.

00:23:07.411 --> 00:23:09.190
But still we can find

00:23:09.191 --> 00:23:12.050
provably optimal sub game
solving techniques,

00:23:12.051 --> 00:23:14.720
so we don't have to look
at the whole game at once,

00:23:14.721 --> 00:23:16.845
and therefore, in sub games,

00:23:16.846 --> 00:23:18.050
we can actually solve

00:23:18.051 --> 00:23:20.510
much finer grain
abstractions come up with

00:23:20.511 --> 00:23:23.080
much better strategies
than you can afford to

00:23:23.081 --> 00:23:24.970
computationally do if you try

00:23:24.971 --> 00:23:27.335
to solve the whole game
monolithically.

00:23:27.336 --> 00:23:28.720
&gt;&gt; Let me ask you something,

00:23:28.721 --> 00:23:31.570
you see how hard it was

00:23:31.571 --> 00:23:34.440
to solve some of
these problems and to get to

00:23:34.441 --> 00:23:36.970
the level of that could be

00:23:36.971 --> 00:23:41.375
a human or set of top notch
humans in this space.

00:23:41.376 --> 00:23:44.350
How on earth did you do
such a good job in this?

00:23:44.351 --> 00:23:45.675
&gt;&gt; That is so amazing.

00:23:45.676 --> 00:23:46.905
That's a great question.

00:23:46.906 --> 00:23:53.020
So, these humans they just
are- I can't even describe it.

00:23:53.021 --> 00:23:55.655
They are so good at
this game which is so hard,

00:23:55.656 --> 00:23:57.010
and they can talk about

00:23:57.011 --> 00:23:58.690
these detailed things some

00:23:58.691 --> 00:24:00.060
of them don't even
have college degrees.

00:24:00.061 --> 00:24:01.135
Many of them actually do, and

00:24:01.136 --> 00:24:03.700
some other computer science,
and economics and so on.

00:24:03.701 --> 00:24:05.590
&gt;&gt; Probably inversely
correlated with their skills.

00:24:05.591 --> 00:24:10.360
&gt;&gt; But they can talk about
this concept that they've

00:24:10.361 --> 00:24:12.319
really learned in
this game specific

00:24:12.320 --> 00:24:14.880
way which is very interesting,

00:24:14.881 --> 00:24:17.560
and they also have
amazing memories about the game.

00:24:17.561 --> 00:24:19.390
So, one guy was

00:24:19.391 --> 00:24:21.345
one of these players would
just finish the match.

00:24:21.346 --> 00:24:26.275
Played 30,000 hands personally
against this AI, and-

00:24:26.276 --> 00:24:28.050
&gt;&gt; Tell me again.
How many of them?

00:24:28.051 --> 00:24:30.170
&gt;&gt; Thirty thousand
hands of poker.

00:24:30.171 --> 00:24:30.760
&gt;&gt; Against.

00:24:30.761 --> 00:24:32.585
&gt;&gt; Against the Hibraro.

00:24:32.586 --> 00:24:35.960
So, overall the four players
played 120,000 hands,

00:24:35.961 --> 00:24:37.900
so we get statistical
significance,

00:24:37.901 --> 00:24:39.620
and to give the human
the best chance

00:24:39.621 --> 00:24:41.085
to find holes in the AI.

00:24:41.086 --> 00:24:43.030
So, you want to play
a lot of hands.

00:24:43.031 --> 00:24:43.805
How long did that take?

00:24:43.806 --> 00:24:51.750
&gt;&gt; Twenty days, so 80 days total
of work done in 20 days.

00:24:51.751 --> 00:24:54.035
Yeah, so he was asked,

00:24:54.036 --> 00:24:56.540
can you come up with
an example where

00:24:56.541 --> 00:24:58.770
the AI exhibit this type of

00:24:58.771 --> 00:25:01.025
behavior and he would recall

00:25:01.026 --> 00:25:04.185
that hand out of
30,000 just like that.

00:25:04.186 --> 00:25:07.900
&gt;&gt; That's amazing. So, I

00:25:07.901 --> 00:25:11.490
mean I'm amazed that
even a intellect can do this.

00:25:11.491 --> 00:25:14.110
Sitting here being burst
situated in this situation.

00:25:14.111 --> 00:25:16.630
Understanding each other's
language abilities or

00:25:16.631 --> 00:25:20.440
you take a constrained games
as complexity like that.

00:25:20.441 --> 00:25:22.515
This human beings
become specialists

00:25:22.516 --> 00:25:25.500
to this they have memory that
indexes into what happened.

00:25:25.501 --> 00:25:25.775
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:25:25.776 --> 00:25:28.430
&gt;&gt; Strategy. It
really is impressive.

00:25:28.431 --> 00:25:30.380
&gt;&gt; Very impressive.

00:25:30.381 --> 00:25:33.120
On the other hand, they

00:25:33.121 --> 00:25:35.365
do nothing but
these all day long.

00:25:35.366 --> 00:25:37.140
So, they do become specialists.

00:25:37.141 --> 00:25:38.980
&gt;&gt; What special
speaking of that topic?

00:25:38.981 --> 00:25:42.450
What about, and I'm
sure that the goal was

00:25:42.451 --> 00:25:46.840
not necessary to beat
the best players at poker,

00:25:46.841 --> 00:25:50.060
it was about to understand
the intelligence better.

00:25:50.061 --> 00:25:53.310
So, what have you
learned about AI in

00:25:53.311 --> 00:25:56.430
the process of working
with [inaudible] and where

00:25:56.431 --> 00:25:59.285
it went per
generalizable principles

00:25:59.286 --> 00:26:01.020
and other kinds of
problems you might

00:26:01.021 --> 00:26:03.020
solve in the world,
in the open world?

00:26:03.021 --> 00:26:04.710
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's a great question.

00:26:04.711 --> 00:26:05.830
So, one is that

00:26:05.831 --> 00:26:07.775
these techniques are not
techniques for poker.

00:26:07.776 --> 00:26:10.605
Therefore, imperfect information
games in general.

00:26:10.606 --> 00:26:13.750
As I think about it,
most real world situations

00:26:13.751 --> 00:26:15.730
are imperfect information games,

00:26:15.731 --> 00:26:17.620
even if they're not
treated as games today.

00:26:17.621 --> 00:26:18.935
&gt;&gt; Give me an example
to make a clear.

00:26:18.936 --> 00:26:21.155
&gt;&gt; Pricing. So, typically,

00:26:21.156 --> 00:26:23.565
when the companies when
they optimize pricing,

00:26:23.566 --> 00:26:25.960
they treat it as
an optimization problem,

00:26:25.961 --> 00:26:28.690
assuming the competitive
prices are fixed.

00:26:28.691 --> 00:26:30.220
Then, when the competitors change

00:26:30.221 --> 00:26:32.425
prices, then you re-optimize.

00:26:32.426 --> 00:26:35.585
You're always in a reactive mode
behind the eight ball.

00:26:35.586 --> 00:26:38.720
While if you think about
these pricing moves ahead,

00:26:38.721 --> 00:26:41.785
you can actually do what
I call strategic pricing.

00:26:41.786 --> 00:26:46.825
This thing happens in a lot
of industries: finance,

00:26:46.826 --> 00:26:51.820
investment banking, sports,
video games, and so on.

00:26:51.821 --> 00:26:54.130
So, this has a lot of
applications and I'm actually so

00:26:54.131 --> 00:26:56.460
excited at that have found
two companies to fill this.

00:26:56.461 --> 00:26:58.085
One is called strategic machine,

00:26:58.086 --> 00:27:00.875
and then for government
application, strategy robots.

00:27:00.876 --> 00:27:02.480
Were taking these
techniques that we've

00:27:02.481 --> 00:27:05.205
developed in the lab
over 16 years now,

00:27:05.206 --> 00:27:09.420
and yeah, filling them into
a bunch of things. So, one.

00:27:09.421 --> 00:27:13.770
I think you do need to think
about imperfect information,

00:27:13.771 --> 00:27:18.095
you do need to deal with
opponents trying to deceive you,

00:27:18.096 --> 00:27:19.725
and so on. So, that's one.

00:27:19.726 --> 00:27:26.535
The other is there's a lot of
talk about oversight of AI.

00:27:26.536 --> 00:27:30.160
We got into this point and
I don't think it's just us,

00:27:30.161 --> 00:27:32.490
I think it's a community as well.

00:27:34.130 --> 00:27:37.810
Whenever human players,
top players even,

00:27:37.811 --> 00:27:40.705
would say, "No,
that's a mistake."

00:27:40.706 --> 00:27:42.935
I would actually know

00:27:42.936 --> 00:27:46.395
the chances are that the human
is wrong, not the AI.

00:27:46.396 --> 00:27:50.170
So, your human oversight
question really became-

00:27:50.171 --> 00:27:51.175
&gt;&gt; Which applications is that?

00:27:51.176 --> 00:27:52.975
&gt;&gt; Poker. I'm
talking about poker.

00:27:52.976 --> 00:27:55.830
So, even the best

00:27:55.831 --> 00:27:57.400
poker players when
they would say, "Okay.

00:27:57.401 --> 00:27:58.625
That was a mistake."

00:27:58.626 --> 00:28:00.700
&gt;&gt; Oh, you mean a poor move?

00:28:00.701 --> 00:28:01.345
&gt;&gt; Poor move.

00:28:01.346 --> 00:28:01.575
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:28:01.576 --> 00:28:02.740
&gt;&gt; Or a poor strategy.

00:28:02.741 --> 00:28:04.860
Sometimes, you can't think
about the moves in isolation,

00:28:04.861 --> 00:28:06.470
you have to think about
whole stretches and moves.

00:28:06.471 --> 00:28:10.105
&gt;&gt; Even with the Alpha ghost
story, we saw examples.

00:28:10.106 --> 00:28:12.410
That's a poor move and
that turned out to

00:28:12.411 --> 00:28:14.010
be unique and interesting.

00:28:14.011 --> 00:28:15.035
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:28:15.036 --> 00:28:16.250
&gt;&gt; You move.

00:28:16.251 --> 00:28:18.710
&gt;&gt; So, sometimes,
these superhuman moves

00:28:18.711 --> 00:28:20.680
or strategies are not

00:28:20.681 --> 00:28:25.330
really easily
evaluable by humans.

00:28:25.331 --> 00:28:28.705
So, when you say that's
a mistake, maybe not.

00:28:28.706 --> 00:28:31.160
I think that's
something as we want to

00:28:31.161 --> 00:28:34.145
get humans to be augmented by AI,

00:28:34.146 --> 00:28:36.780
we need to think about how
do we deal with the fact

00:28:36.781 --> 00:28:40.105
that humans can't really provide
proper oversight for AI,

00:28:40.106 --> 00:28:41.630
and AI may also have

00:28:41.631 --> 00:28:44.350
its own weaknesses in
providing oversight to humans.

00:28:44.351 --> 00:28:46.775
So, there should be
this mutual oversight.

00:28:46.776 --> 00:28:48.665
But how do you do
that really when

00:28:48.666 --> 00:28:52.200
the humans can't evaluate
superhuman moves?

00:28:52.201 --> 00:28:53.570
&gt;&gt; So, where are you?

00:28:53.571 --> 00:28:56.560
You're taking on
directions there.

00:28:56.561 --> 00:28:58.875
Feel free to speculate
in real time

00:28:58.876 --> 00:29:01.185
if you haven't solved
the problem yet.

00:29:01.186 --> 00:29:03.890
&gt;&gt; Okay. Well, I

00:29:04.040 --> 00:29:07.820
actually don't really think

00:29:07.821 --> 00:29:09.490
that humans can provide

00:29:09.491 --> 00:29:11.310
very good oversight for
AI in the long run,

00:29:11.311 --> 00:29:14.695
because they don't
understand superhuman moves.

00:29:14.696 --> 00:29:21.935
So, that's one, and other
is that for a while,

00:29:21.936 --> 00:29:23.585
as you get superhuman moves,

00:29:23.586 --> 00:29:26.270
you can give humans
extra data around

00:29:26.271 --> 00:29:28.200
that decision and give them

00:29:28.201 --> 00:29:31.290
time to understand them
and evaluate them,

00:29:31.291 --> 00:29:33.720
and try to understand
why wasn't it good,

00:29:33.721 --> 00:29:35.300
why was it bad, and so on.

00:29:35.301 --> 00:29:38.100
We actually did
this. Chris Ferguson

00:29:38.101 --> 00:29:39.860
was looking at our moves

00:29:39.861 --> 00:29:43.590
sometimes offline where
something looked like

00:29:43.591 --> 00:29:45.370
a really bad move to me

00:29:45.371 --> 00:29:47.150
or to one of
the top poker players.

00:29:47.151 --> 00:29:48.740
So, we would actually isolate it.

00:29:48.741 --> 00:29:50.870
Okay. At that point,

00:29:50.871 --> 00:29:53.795
here where the belief
distributions of the AI,

00:29:53.796 --> 00:29:55.935
here's the belief
distributions that they

00:29:55.936 --> 00:29:58.790
would've had if it were
in the human shoes,

00:29:58.791 --> 00:30:02.340
and from these belief
distributions,

00:30:02.341 --> 00:30:04.125
was that a good play?

00:30:04.126 --> 00:30:06.290
With three days of analysis and

00:30:06.291 --> 00:30:07.655
oftentimes a little essay

00:30:07.656 --> 00:30:09.110
to go with it,
Chris was able say,

00:30:09.111 --> 00:30:11.280
it actually looked
like a dumb move,

00:30:11.281 --> 00:30:13.150
but it actually is
like thinking two

00:30:13.151 --> 00:30:15.510
levels deeper than
humans ever do,

00:30:15.511 --> 00:30:16.965
and it's actually
a brilliant move.

00:30:16.966 --> 00:30:20.845
So, with some hindsight and
time and extra context,

00:30:20.846 --> 00:30:22.590
humans actually are able to

00:30:22.591 --> 00:30:25.500
appreciate and evaluate
superhuman moves.

00:30:25.501 --> 00:30:26.870
But I think that'll go away,

00:30:26.871 --> 00:30:28.730
that'll only go for a while when

00:30:28.731 --> 00:30:31.485
the superhuman moves
aren't too superhuman yet.

00:30:31.486 --> 00:30:32.780
But once you get beyond

00:30:32.781 --> 00:30:34.370
a certain point,
I don't think so.

00:30:34.371 --> 00:30:36.945
&gt;&gt; In the open world,
we have to assume that

00:30:36.946 --> 00:30:42.835
some moves advertised as
superhuman might be erroneous,

00:30:42.836 --> 00:30:44.000
might be actually wrong.

00:30:44.001 --> 00:30:44.615
&gt;&gt; That's right.

00:30:44.616 --> 00:30:46.630
&gt;&gt; So, people get used
to accepting moves they

00:30:46.631 --> 00:30:48.910
don't understand,
the opacity there.

00:30:48.911 --> 00:30:50.985
It's unclear what
the policy should be about

00:30:50.986 --> 00:30:53.575
humans actually providing
some oversight.

00:30:53.576 --> 00:30:54.160
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:30:54.161 --> 00:30:55.535
&gt;&gt; Be able to stop things or

00:30:55.536 --> 00:30:56.900
sometimes, we'll modify them.

00:30:56.901 --> 00:30:58.500
&gt;&gt; Yeah. There's also,

00:30:58.501 --> 00:30:59.670
if you think about things like

00:30:59.671 --> 00:31:01.125
robots where you have to do in

00:31:01.126 --> 00:31:02.330
real time in order to take

00:31:02.331 --> 00:31:03.810
over the controls or don't you,

00:31:03.811 --> 00:31:05.960
and the AI might
have driven you into

00:31:05.961 --> 00:31:09.610
a situation where it can still
recover but a human can't.

00:31:09.611 --> 00:31:11.820
&gt;&gt; Because a human doesn't
go into those states

00:31:11.821 --> 00:31:14.040
base parts and so on. So, it's
a very complicated thing.

00:31:14.041 --> 00:31:17.150
&gt;&gt; So, once we have validation
and some notion of trust,

00:31:17.151 --> 00:31:23.320
it might turn out that we
have to give robotic systems,

00:31:23.321 --> 00:31:26.070
automated systems,
the ability to download.

00:31:26.071 --> 00:31:27.400
So, they're doing things that are

00:31:27.401 --> 00:31:28.890
understandable to human beings.

00:31:28.891 --> 00:31:35.220
So, they can be understood
and guided if necessary.

00:31:35.221 --> 00:31:35.755
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:31:35.756 --> 00:31:39.195
&gt;&gt; Which might be a gentle trade
between two optimality

00:31:39.196 --> 00:31:40.820
and winning a game for

00:31:40.821 --> 00:31:43.330
example and just
doing a great job.

00:31:43.331 --> 00:31:45.330
&gt;&gt; Yeah. I do think, yeah,

00:31:45.331 --> 00:31:48.120
this bias, in some applications
might be a gentle trade.

00:31:48.121 --> 00:31:51.340
Like, hey you're going to take
over the wheel in the car?

00:31:51.341 --> 00:31:53.240
Yeah, that's probably
a gentle trade.

00:31:53.241 --> 00:31:55.660
So, but in other applications,

00:31:55.661 --> 00:31:58.480
I think the tradeoff
won't be that gentle.

00:31:58.481 --> 00:32:00.500
We may have to give up in

00:32:00.501 --> 00:32:02.860
some applications,
human understandbility.

00:32:02.861 --> 00:32:04.485
&gt;&gt; Certainly, you might
not win a poker game.

00:32:04.486 --> 00:32:05.074
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:32:05.075 --> 00:32:05.865
&gt;&gt; If you give up these moves.

00:32:05.866 --> 00:32:06.340
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:32:06.341 --> 00:32:07.780
&gt;&gt; Interesting. So, tell

00:32:07.781 --> 00:32:09.120
me a little bit
about strategy robot

00:32:09.121 --> 00:32:10.420
and why this interest in

00:32:10.421 --> 00:32:12.780
helping governments
with their projects.

00:32:12.781 --> 00:32:14.910
&gt;&gt; Well, actually, I

00:32:14.911 --> 00:32:17.160
started it with
a strategic machine first,

00:32:17.161 --> 00:32:18.930
which was really for everything,

00:32:18.931 --> 00:32:20.000
and then it turned out that

00:32:20.001 --> 00:32:23.710
the potential customer base

00:32:23.711 --> 00:32:24.975
is a totally different

00:32:24.976 --> 00:32:26.660
and other requirements
are different.

00:32:26.661 --> 00:32:28.365
So, I actually had
to split it in two.

00:32:28.366 --> 00:32:30.660
One for the commercial
side strategic machine,

00:32:30.661 --> 00:32:32.100
and then one for
the government things

00:32:32.101 --> 00:32:33.670
which is strategy robot.

00:32:33.671 --> 00:32:35.750
&gt;&gt; Tell me more about
the government work.

00:32:35.751 --> 00:32:38.370
What problems are
you solving there?

00:32:38.371 --> 00:32:40.910
&gt;&gt; So far, we are targeting

00:32:40.911 --> 00:32:44.085
things like military planning,

00:32:44.086 --> 00:32:49.690
cyber security
applications, intelligence,

00:32:49.691 --> 00:32:52.170
war gaming, training,
things like that.

00:32:52.171 --> 00:32:56.890
&gt;&gt; Very interesting.
The whole area of AI,

00:32:56.891 --> 00:32:58.310
you've been in for
many years and it's

00:32:58.311 --> 00:33:00.410
fabulous and it's both

00:33:00.411 --> 00:33:03.570
involved in this wonderful
long-term mission

00:33:03.571 --> 00:33:05.860
to understand intelligence.

00:33:05.861 --> 00:33:07.720
There are different perspectives,

00:33:07.721 --> 00:33:09.425
different approaches,
different methodologies,

00:33:09.426 --> 00:33:14.890
but certainly a big difference
in point of perspective,

00:33:14.891 --> 00:33:18.480
is that people who work
on central algorithms

00:33:18.481 --> 00:33:21.640
and deeply coordinated
approaches and the people

00:33:21.641 --> 00:33:28.670
that often rely on the power
of distribution methods,

00:33:28.671 --> 00:33:34.610
theorems, equilibria, seeking
intelligence in a system

00:33:34.611 --> 00:33:37.120
as the sum of many parts

00:33:37.121 --> 00:33:39.150
that have been interacting
in certain properties.

00:33:39.151 --> 00:33:41.215
I'm curious where you are
these days in your thinking

00:33:41.216 --> 00:33:46.180
about the foundations
of human intelligence,

00:33:46.181 --> 00:33:48.420
the importance of
central coordination

00:33:48.421 --> 00:33:50.860
versus distributed processing.

00:33:50.861 --> 00:33:53.320
&gt;&gt; Okay. That's a big question.

00:33:53.321 --> 00:33:54.980
&gt;&gt; I hardly ask little questions,

00:33:54.981 --> 00:33:56.300
Well, I guess I did
a couple a few minutes ago.

00:33:56.301 --> 00:33:57.460
This is a big one, yes.

00:33:57.461 --> 00:34:04.105
&gt;&gt; Okay. So, I started
out by thinking at

00:34:04.106 --> 00:34:07.545
part of the reason
for distribution

00:34:07.546 --> 00:34:12.165
is autonomy and part of it
is computational efficiency,

00:34:12.166 --> 00:34:15.710
and those are the distribution
is the same for that tool.

00:34:15.711 --> 00:34:18.700
In the track on a system
that's an example where

00:34:18.701 --> 00:34:20.110
we did distributed negotiation

00:34:20.111 --> 00:34:22.285
peer to peer,
totally distributed.

00:34:22.286 --> 00:34:23.440
But then when I moved to

00:34:23.441 --> 00:34:25.995
combinatorial auctions around 97,

00:34:25.996 --> 00:34:28.925
it became clear that
you can actually get

00:34:28.926 --> 00:34:31.480
better allocations
and more efficiently

00:34:31.481 --> 00:34:33.415
by having a centralized
clearing point,

00:34:33.416 --> 00:34:34.915
like an auction server,

00:34:34.916 --> 00:34:36.715
combinatorial auction server with

00:34:36.716 --> 00:34:40.370
centralized optimization
algorithms to end allocation.

00:34:40.371 --> 00:34:43.700
So, then, there was
this autonomy notion of

00:34:43.701 --> 00:34:47.400
different bidders but it wasn't

00:34:47.401 --> 00:34:49.735
from a computational perspective.

00:34:49.736 --> 00:34:51.480
From a computational perspective,

00:34:51.481 --> 00:34:53.695
it was actually
better to centralize.

00:34:53.696 --> 00:34:57.825
Now, if you think
about how that works,

00:34:57.826 --> 00:35:00.780
for example, we built
our own hosting services,

00:35:00.781 --> 00:35:03.590
we were a Software as
a Service company,

00:35:03.591 --> 00:35:04.730
one of the first analytics

00:35:04.731 --> 00:35:06.110
software and services companies,

00:35:06.111 --> 00:35:07.485
but today it would be cloud,

00:35:07.486 --> 00:35:09.780
and is at optimized markets,

00:35:09.781 --> 00:35:13.590
which is a combinatorial market
company that I'm running.

00:35:13.591 --> 00:35:19.420
Their autonomy
based distribution,

00:35:19.421 --> 00:35:21.910
self-interest based distribution,
is totally different

00:35:21.911 --> 00:35:23.760
than the computational
distribution.

00:35:23.761 --> 00:35:26.100
In that from the
player's perspective,

00:35:26.101 --> 00:35:27.825
it's a centralized marketplace

00:35:27.826 --> 00:35:30.210
while from a computational
perspective,

00:35:30.211 --> 00:35:32.210
the clearing engine or

00:35:32.211 --> 00:35:34.365
the marketplace
driving optimization,

00:35:34.366 --> 00:35:37.100
that is distributed in the cloud
across multiple servers

00:35:37.101 --> 00:35:40.070
but those are all in
the centralized clearing point.

00:35:40.071 --> 00:35:42.570
So, the distribution for autonomy

00:35:42.571 --> 00:35:44.580
and the distribution of
computational speed,

00:35:44.581 --> 00:35:45.830
they're totally different.

00:35:45.831 --> 00:35:47.180
They're both in
the same market at

00:35:47.181 --> 00:35:49.235
the same time but
they're different.

00:35:49.236 --> 00:35:51.010
&gt;&gt; So, there's no summering

00:35:51.011 --> 00:35:52.280
answer or do you think
that you have to

00:35:52.281 --> 00:35:56.370
have that intellect on
both kinds of processes,

00:35:56.371 --> 00:35:57.640
coordination might always be

00:35:57.641 --> 00:36:00.770
necessary at some level,
more centralized?

00:36:00.771 --> 00:36:03.390
&gt;&gt; I won't to say it's
always necessary but

00:36:03.391 --> 00:36:06.790
especially when it comes to
these combinatorial problems,

00:36:06.791 --> 00:36:10.575
getting good solutions, of
let's say optimal solutions

00:36:10.576 --> 00:36:12.310
or near optimal solutions in

00:36:12.311 --> 00:36:14.605
these combinatorial
allocation problems.

00:36:14.606 --> 00:36:16.810
It is very hard to
do in a peer to

00:36:16.811 --> 00:36:19.840
peer way without
a centralized clearing point.

00:36:19.841 --> 00:36:21.810
So, maybe just my experience,

00:36:21.811 --> 00:36:23.430
tracking being the least sexy but

00:36:23.431 --> 00:36:25.790
most combinatorial problem ever,

00:36:25.791 --> 00:36:28.935
that's maybe driving
my thinking off that.

00:36:28.936 --> 00:36:32.360
But just the way we saw the
more combinatorics there are,

00:36:32.361 --> 00:36:35.830
the more benefit there is to
a centralized clearing point.

00:36:35.831 --> 00:36:37.680
&gt;&gt; You know your
experience Thomas,

00:36:37.681 --> 00:36:39.280
I don't usually ask
this question to folks

00:36:39.281 --> 00:36:42.995
but I thought I'll get you
to speculate in real time.

00:36:42.996 --> 00:36:45.550
What do you think it's
going on in the human mind?

00:36:45.551 --> 00:36:49.550
What what's your
favorite- so give me

00:36:49.551 --> 00:36:51.450
your intuitions about
what might be going on

00:36:51.451 --> 00:36:54.730
that performs all this magic?

00:36:55.560 --> 00:36:58.680
&gt;&gt; I made a commitment
fairly early on

00:36:58.681 --> 00:37:00.490
not to worry about it too much.

00:37:00.491 --> 00:37:02.720
&gt;&gt; I didn't say worry, I
said what's your best bet,

00:37:02.721 --> 00:37:04.660
you must have some guesses and

00:37:04.661 --> 00:37:07.325
some inspiration,
some thoughts insights.

00:37:07.326 --> 00:37:09.760
Even if we know they're
just guesses right now.

00:37:09.761 --> 00:37:12.360
Because sometimes these guesses
guide our research,

00:37:12.361 --> 00:37:15.820
even though we don't talk
about this very much.

00:37:17.450 --> 00:37:20.860
&gt;&gt; Maybe less so. Actually, when

00:37:20.861 --> 00:37:22.855
I was picking a research
topic in the 80s,

00:37:22.856 --> 00:37:26.120
one of the other areas I
was very interested in

00:37:26.121 --> 00:37:29.500
I had done some things was
deep learning and I guess-.

00:37:29.501 --> 00:37:31.720
&gt;&gt; The connection is that in
those days as we called it.

00:37:31.721 --> 00:37:33.755
&gt;&gt; Yes. Neural networks.

00:37:33.756 --> 00:37:36.110
I was very interested in that,

00:37:36.111 --> 00:37:38.725
and just by half fate,

00:37:38.726 --> 00:37:40.180
this is more than the game

00:37:40.181 --> 00:37:42.285
theoretically strand is
more kind of where I went,

00:37:42.286 --> 00:37:44.450
and for better or worse,

00:37:44.451 --> 00:37:46.150
it's been an interesting ride.

00:37:46.151 --> 00:37:47.890
But I don't know so

00:37:47.891 --> 00:37:49.675
much about what's going
in the human mind.

00:37:49.676 --> 00:37:51.355
&gt;&gt;But it sounds like

00:37:51.356 --> 00:37:52.950
that was an early
curiosity of yours?

00:37:52.951 --> 00:37:53.760
&gt;&gt; Yes. Very much.

00:37:53.761 --> 00:37:55.515
&gt;&gt; Maybe even powered up
the whole AI direction?

00:37:55.516 --> 00:37:56.840
&gt;&gt; Yes. Very much.

00:37:56.841 --> 00:37:58.390
&gt;&gt; So, I'll keep it
that right now and we

00:37:58.391 --> 00:38:00.305
won't speculate
especially on video.

00:38:00.306 --> 00:38:01.485
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:38:01.486 --> 00:38:03.530
&gt;&gt; Turning to other people,

00:38:03.531 --> 00:38:04.750
you've been a great mentor to

00:38:04.751 --> 00:38:06.160
many students over the years,

00:38:06.161 --> 00:38:10.240
and you've had
a very successful career

00:38:10.241 --> 00:38:12.065
and much more to come.

00:38:12.066 --> 00:38:15.025
What are some words of wisdom
you would like to share

00:38:15.026 --> 00:38:17.660
with young folks about career,

00:38:17.661 --> 00:38:18.990
more generally even inside

00:38:18.991 --> 00:38:20.850
or outside of computer science?

00:38:20.851 --> 00:38:23.300
&gt;&gt; Okay. Interesting.

00:38:23.301 --> 00:38:27.820
&gt;&gt; I know you probably will
never in video or anywhere

00:38:27.821 --> 00:38:32.840
else be asked those two different
questions side by side.

00:38:32.841 --> 00:38:36.205
Brain works and your thoughts
are there and then-

00:38:36.206 --> 00:38:38.860
&gt;&gt; In computer science,
my advice would be

00:38:38.861 --> 00:38:41.260
make sure you have
some real world problem

00:38:41.261 --> 00:38:41.910
that you work on

00:38:41.911 --> 00:38:44.843
because it will fuel a lot of

00:38:44.844 --> 00:38:46.290
interesting research questions

00:38:46.291 --> 00:38:48.935
into the research agenda.

00:38:48.936 --> 00:38:52.710
It's very hard to come up
with those questions that are

00:38:52.711 --> 00:38:55.400
significant if you're just
sitting in your office

00:38:55.401 --> 00:38:56.655
with your feet on table or trying

00:38:56.656 --> 00:38:58.345
to flip through proceedings.

00:38:58.346 --> 00:39:00.390
So, it's better to be
the person who publishes

00:39:00.391 --> 00:39:03.140
the first article on some topic,

00:39:03.141 --> 00:39:07.410
and brings a new question
into the field rather

00:39:07.411 --> 00:39:09.330
than being the guy
who flips through

00:39:09.331 --> 00:39:12.085
the proceedings looking for
potential improvements.

00:39:12.086 --> 00:39:14.560
That's my opinion. We've
done some of the latter too,

00:39:14.561 --> 00:39:18.140
but I think that's
been less impactful.

00:39:18.141 --> 00:39:22.540
I think interdisciplinary,

00:39:22.541 --> 00:39:26.390
so I foundthat as being
great to be able to

00:39:26.391 --> 00:39:30.065
draw knowledge from
multiple different fields.

00:39:30.066 --> 00:39:31.940
So, one example is
the game theory

00:39:31.941 --> 00:39:33.690
and optimization intersection,

00:39:33.691 --> 00:39:36.230
and other is really
the intersection between

00:39:36.231 --> 00:39:39.600
AI techniques and
integer programming techniques.

00:39:39.601 --> 00:39:42.690
These fields didn't really
talk to each other much,

00:39:42.691 --> 00:39:45.470
and this was
tremendously helpful.

00:39:45.471 --> 00:39:48.530
Now, they talk to
each other much more

00:39:48.531 --> 00:39:50.590
but it was really great to
actually know the techniques

00:39:50.591 --> 00:39:52.540
from both sides and
being able to contribute

00:39:52.541 --> 00:39:57.525
to that intersection with
both types of toolsets in hand.

00:39:57.526 --> 00:40:02.430
So, just reading on both sides,

00:40:02.431 --> 00:40:06.885
reading broadly and not
drawing boundaries.

00:40:06.886 --> 00:40:09.595
Instead of thinking about it in

00:40:09.596 --> 00:40:10.940
a paper I sometimes see people

00:40:10.941 --> 00:40:12.475
write to computer
scientists think this,

00:40:12.476 --> 00:40:15.375
economists think that
or things like that,

00:40:15.376 --> 00:40:18.560
I would never mention a field
of science in a paper,

00:40:18.561 --> 00:40:20.835
and I encourage that
nobody else does either

00:40:20.836 --> 00:40:23.470
because that erects
boundaries between fields,

00:40:23.471 --> 00:40:24.750
instead of taking them down.

00:40:24.751 --> 00:40:26.885
It doesn't really matter
whether it was an economist or

00:40:26.886 --> 00:40:29.900
computer scientist or an OR
person who said this or that.

00:40:29.901 --> 00:40:31.020
That's really irrelevant,

00:40:31.021 --> 00:40:32.690
and a waste of space in a paper.

00:40:32.691 --> 00:40:34.925
&gt;&gt; Fabulous. Well.
Thanks very much.

00:40:34.926 --> 00:40:35.555
It's been great talking to you.

00:40:35.556 --> 00:40:36.940
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

